1
00:00:35,860 --> 00:00:38,200
CONVERSATION 1
How's your dissertation going?

2
00:00:38,200 --> 00:00:43,140
I'm proofreading my first draft and will submit it to my professor tomorrow.

3
00:00:43,140 --> 00:00:50,720
Oh, I haven't even started writing mine yet, so I'm really worried about finishing by the end of next semester.

4
00:00:50,720 --> 00:00:53,660
You mean you haven't even begun yours yet?

5
00:00:53,660 --> 00:00:55,720
The final draft is due in five months.

6
00:00:55,720 --> 00:01:05,860
Of course I've started it, but I can't get to the writing yet as I haven't found enough resources to use, so I'm still researching the topic.

7
00:01:05,860 --> 00:01:09,260
Maybe the problem is the way you're doing your research.

8
00:01:09,260 --> 00:01:13,540
I started by talking to my professor about where to look for information.

9
00:01:13,540 --> 00:01:21,040
And based on that, I found books in the library and a lot of reputable journal articles on the Internet.

10
00:01:21,040 --> 00:01:29,860
I've tried all that, but don't have enough to write the dissertation as my department's minimum length is 70 pages.

11
00:01:29,860 --> 00:01:33,900
I think the problem is that my topic isn't viable.

12
00:01:33,900 --> 00:01:40,700
And honestly, my professor did warn me at the beginning that I might not be able to find enough material.

13
00:01:40,700 --> 00:01:45,780
But I was so interested in the topic that I didn't let his advice deter me.

14
00:01:45,780 --> 00:01:48,700
Well, I suggest you find a new topic.

15
00:01:48,700 --> 00:01:54,040
After all, our professors are here to guide us, so it's best to listen to them.

16
00:01:54,040 --> 00:01:58,740
In retrospect, I wish I had listened to him, but I didn't.

17
00:01:58,740 --> 00:02:04,580
And now I don't want to give up my topic as I've already invested so much time and energy.

18
00:02:04,580 --> 00:02:11,640
If you're committed to your current topic, maybe you could make some adjustments rather than abandon it completely.

19
00:02:11,640 --> 00:02:12,560
What is your topic?

20
00:02:12,560 --> 00:02:18,460
It's "Depictions of Femininity and Folklore from the South of the Country".

21
00:02:18,460 --> 00:02:20,120
That's pretty narrow.

22
00:02:20,120 --> 00:02:26,460
You could find more material if you made the topic broader, maybe by including other kinds of depictions.

23
00:02:26,460 --> 00:02:28,980
Broadening the topic is a great idea.

24
00:02:28,980 --> 00:02:39,820
I'll start by including folklore from other regions of the country.

25
00:02:39,820 --> 00:02:57,320
Q1. What does the woman say about her dissertation?

26
00:02:57,320 --> 00:03:14,640
Q2. What does the man say about his professor?

27
00:03:14,640 --> 00:03:31,420
Q3. What does the woman say about her professor?

28
00:03:31,420 --> 00:03:51,020
Q4. What do we learn the woman will do to complete her dissertation?

29
00:03:51,020 --> 00:04:02,160
CONVERSATION 2
Today, on Book Talk, we are lucky enough to host John Robbins and discuss his new book, Why Americans are Fat and How We Can Lose Weight.

30
00:04:02,160 --> 00:04:10,120
John isn't just a respected writer; he's also one of the rare celebrity authors writing about science today.

31
00:04:10,120 --> 00:04:14,340
Thanks for having me, Rebecca, but I'm hardly a celebrity.

32
00:04:14,340 --> 00:04:25,660
That's very modest of you to say, considering that your four books have sold a total of seven million copies worldwide, and they've been translated into 12 different languages.

33
00:04:25,660 --> 00:04:29,580
What makes people so fascinated with your work?

34
00:04:29,580 --> 00:04:36,840
Well, people read my books because more than 60% of Americans are overweight or obese.

35
00:04:36,840 --> 00:04:40,000
And other countries are facing similar problems.

36
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:43,220
Basically, we all want to know how to fix things.

37
00:04:43,220 --> 00:04:44,940
We certainly do.

38
00:04:44,940 --> 00:04:52,820
I've read your new book and it's fabulous, especially when it comes to the way you make difficult science easy for laymen to understand.

39
00:04:52,820 --> 00:04:56,080
That's no small achievement.

40
00:04:56,080 --> 00:05:07,040
I'm glad to hear you find my work accessible, because I was worried when I wrote it that discussing the science might make the book more suited for a specialist audience.

41
00:05:07,040 --> 00:05:10,620
My last book was written primarily for the medical community.

42
00:05:10,620 --> 00:05:16,400
But this time, I want to help ordinary people take control of their weight.

43
00:05:16,400 --> 00:05:18,160
And how do you suggest they do that?

44
00:05:18,160 --> 00:05:23,540
Can you give us the basics of your advice for people who want to lose weight?

45
00:05:23,540 --> 00:05:31,580
Briefly, I argue that every person needs to consider their metabolism and eat what suits their body's needs.

46
00:05:31,580 --> 00:05:34,480
I don't advocate one single diet.

47
00:05:34,480 --> 00:05:37,440
Some people should eat more carbohydrates than others.

48
00:05:37,440 --> 00:05:40,460
And different people need different amounts of protein and fat.

49
00:05:40,460 --> 00:05:49,920
But you do have some recommendations for everyone, including eating ten servings of vegetables and three of fruit a day.

50
00:05:49,920 --> 00:06:04,080
We'll talk about those recommendations next, but now we need to take a short break for a message from our sponsor.

51
00:06:04,080 --> 00:06:22,120
Q5. What does the woman say about the man in her introduction?

52
00:06:22,120 --> 00:06:39,980
Q6. What has motivated the man to write his books?

53
00:06:49,980 --> 00:06:57,420
Q7. Who does the man say his last book was mainly written for?

54
00:06:57,420 --> 00:07:42,440
Q8. What does the man recommend people do?

55
00:07:42,440 --> 00:07:52,940
PASSAGE 1
Stress is often depicted as negative, but research shows that moderate amounts of it can be beneficial for your brain and your body.

56
00:07:52,940 --> 00:07:56,840
First, the benefits for the brain.

57
00:07:56,840 --> 00:08:02,960
Studies have shown that short periods of stress can actually bolster cognitive functioning.

58
00:08:02,960 --> 00:08:12,160
Researchers discovered that placing rats in a stressful situation for just a few hours doubled the growth of new ) brain cells.

59
00:08:12,160 --> 00:08:16,940
The rats also did better on a memory test later on.

60
00:08:16,940 --> 00:08:21,220
Scientists think the same thing happens in humans.

61
00:08:21,220 --> 00:08:25,200
But how does stress improve memory?

62
00:08:25,200 --> 00:08:26,540
It's simple.

63
00:08:26,540 --> 00:08:31,260
When your brain cells multiply, your memory can improve.

64
00:08:31,260 --> 00:08:41,180
Viewed from a biological perspective, this makes sense, ( because animals that are better at remembering dangerous situations can avoid them in the future.

65
00:08:41,180 --> 00:08:51,240
If an animal encounters a predator and escapes, for example, it's important to remember where and when that encounter happened.

66
00:08:51,240 --> 00:08:55,380
Experts assert that the same principle applies to humans.

67
00:08:55,380 --> 00:08:59,880
Now, let's turn to how stress benefits the body.

68
00:08:59,880 --> 00:09:02,500
This may come as a surprise to laymen.

69
00:09:02,500 --> 00:09:07,720
But experts say that stress can keep you from getting sick.

70
00:09:07,720 --> 00:09:12,140
Scientists concede that chronic stress can make you more prone to illness.

71
00:09:12,140 --> 00:09:23,680
But research shows that short periods of stress can actually provide some protection against getting sick, because it increases your immune functioning.

72
00:09:23,680 --> 00:09:35,020
One study shows that rats that experienced brief stress had a surge of immune cell response, which makes the immune system better prepared to fight illness.

73
00:09:35,020 --> 00:09:51,720
For humans, there's even evidence that experiencing stress before getting vaccinated could help make vaccines more effective.

74
00:09:51,720 --> 00:10:11,960
Q9. What did researchers discover about rats placed briefly in a stressful situation?

75
00:10:11,960 --> 00:10:30,820
Q10. Why do people tend to have clear memories of dangerous situations they have encountered?

76
00:10:30,820 --> 00:10:49,820
Q11. What do scientists believe chronic stress can do?

77
00:10:49,820 --> 00:10:56,040
PASSAGE 2
For many managers and people who work in leadership positions, dealing with emails is a dilemma.

78
00:10:56,040 --> 00:11:13,040
It's likely the unpredictable, uncontrollable and ongoing nature of day-to-day email in terms of volume, importance and urgency contributes to their levels of anxiety and to diminished leadership skills.

79
00:11:13,040 --> 00:11:19,880
That's because it's not unusual for many leaders to prioritize email management over people management.

80
00:11:19,880 --> 00:11:27,040
An obsession with managing their inbox prevents them from dealing with their employees.

81
00:11:27,040 --> 00:11:41,240
As a result, they ignore the issues that might only be mild problems at first, until unfortunately, they inevitably transform into a major problem or crisis by virtue of neglect.

82
00:11:41,240 --> 00:12:01,360
As leaders, they are expected to motivate and inspire their team in pursuit of longer term strategic goals and also, less ambitiously but more practically, to monitor their daily output, to set clear expectations, and to give regular feedback.

83
00:12:01,360 --> 00:12:10,220
When presented with a choice between the appeal of their inbox and other more important activities, many sacrifice the latter.

84
00:12:10,220 --> 00:12:14,240
Daily email demands have a negative impact on their goal progress.

85
00:12:14,240 --> 00:12:24,020
This is because leaders must divert resources from other tasks to check, filter and respond to emails.

86
00:12:24,020 --> 00:12:33,840
The solution is cultivating self-control which is like a muscle-it can be strengthened or improved over time through exercise.

87
00:12:33,840 --> 00:13:00,800
Some suggestions include making space in your diary for the only periods during which you'll be checking emails, setting a timer for yourself so you don't become distracted by your inbox for too long, turning off email alerts so you're not interrupted by them.

88
00:13:00,800 --> 00:13:19,100
Q12. What does the speaker say is a common problem with managers?

89
00:13:19,100 --> 00:13:37,080
Q13. What may happen when managers ignore minor problems?

90
00:13:37,080 --> 00:13:55,800
Q14. What are leaders expected to do in pursuing their strategic goals?

91
00:13:55,800 --> 00:14:41,400
Q15. How can a manager best avoid being distracted by email?

92
00:14:41,400 --> 00:14:52,060
RECORDING 1
In last week's lecture, we talked about the problems caused by poor eating habits, focusing on how medical professionals are trying to solve these problems.

93
00:14:52,060 --> 00:14:59,980
Today, we'll continue with the topic, but focus on research from a different field-marketing.

94
00:14:59,980 --> 00:15:04,440
Now, what can marketing tell us about improving nutrition?

95
00:15:04,440 --> 00:15:14,020
Well, a team of marketing professors has studied the importance of the visual aspect of food and how that influences food choices.

96
00:15:14,020 --> 00:15:25,080
Those marketing experts assert that the impact of the appearance of food is greater than we might presume, and might hold the key to encouraging better eating.

97
00:15:25,080 --> 00:15:29,600
So how important is the appearance of food?

98
00:15:29,600 --> 00:15:42,060
Research shows that just seeing an appealing photograph of a hamburger in an advertisement, for example, can cause individuals to imagine the taste or smell of that hamburger.

99
00:15:42,060 --> 00:15:45,700
This can make them more likely to purchase and consume it.

100
00:15:45,700 --> 00:15:49,760
Of course, that's the point of advertisements.

101
00:15:49,760 --> 00:15:52,620
So isn't that a good thing?

102
00:15:52,620 --> 00:16:08,360
Well, the marketing professors argue that this is actually a problem, because the combination of pleasing aesthetics of and easy access to unhealthy foods, such as hamburgers and pizza, may be contributing to a worldwide health crisis.

103
00:16:08,360 --> 00:16:20,380
In fact, statistics show that 39% of all adults in the world weigh too much and another 13% are obese.

104
00:16:20,380 --> 00:16:36,460
Now, as we discussed last time, medical professionals have tried to fight the obesity epidemic by focusing on the nutrients found in different foods, emphasizing things like carbohydrate, fat, sugar and calorie content.

105
00:16:36,460 --> 00:16:39,440
But they've had limited success.

106
00:16:39,440 --> 00:16:51,760
The marketing professors believe that the real solution to obesity is making food look appealing and focusing on the pleasure of both looking at and eating that food.

107
00:16:51,760 --> 00:16:56,880
That pleasure can be used as a tool to promote healthy food choices.

108
00:16:56,880 --> 00:17:11,040
In fact, the research done by the team showed that associating healthy food with pleasant images, experiences and emotions led to greater interest in purchasing or eating it.

109
00:17:11,040 --> 00:17:18,200
"This is a better strategy," they claim, then reminding consumers that a certain food is good for them.

110
00:17:18,200 --> 00:17:20,560
But are they right?

111
00:17:20,560 --> 00:17:26,960
Well, the researchers cited a marketing campaign designed to reverse the sales decline of carrots.

112
00:17:26,960 --> 00:17:36,720
The campaign didn't emphasize the carrots' healthy qualities, but embrace their beauty and the pleasure derived from eating them.

113
00:17:36,720 --> 00:17:44,260
For example, the ads focused on the bright, beautiful orange color and crisp texture of the carrots.

114
00:17:44,260 --> 00:17:50,520
The campaign led to an impressive increase in product sales of more than 10%.

115
00:17:50,520 --> 00:18:11,477
The researchers believe that other companies could bolster sales of healthy foods with similar ads, depicting their products as attractive and a source of pleasure.

116
00:18:11,477 --> 00:18:29,080
Q16. What does the speaker say a team of marketing professors has studied?

117
00:18:29,080 --> 00:18:47,620
Q17. How have medical professionals tried to fight obesity?

118
00:18:47,620 --> 00:19:07,220
Q18. What can other companies learn from the example of the carrot promotion campaign?

119
00:19:07,220 --> 00:19:14,460
RECORDING 2
The household cleaning products industry really began to take off in the 1950s.

120
00:19:14,460 --> 00:19:26,900
Prior to that resourceful housewives, who had grown up in a less prosperous era, relied on substances that they used in their kitchens such as vinegar used to clean windows.

121
00:19:26,900 --> 00:19:36,180
Today, the household cleaning products industry is worth billions of dollars a year and is dominated by large global companies.

122
00:19:36,180 --> 00:19:49,860
In recent years, however, many countries have witnessed a surge in the number of small businesses and companies offering more environment-friendly cleaning products, and they're doing good business, too.

123
00:19:49,860 --> 00:19:57,600
One reason for this is that consumers are becoming more aware of environment and sustainability issues.

124
00:19:57,600 --> 00:20:03,660
They also want to make their home a safe place, free from toxic chemicals.

125
00:20:03,660 --> 00:20:10,780
The third reason is people's awareness of a knowledge about technology has never been greater.

126
00:20:10,780 --> 00:20:26,200
In the past, people might have been skeptical of a new product claimed to be able to achieve amazing cleaning results, but they now have faith in technology's ability to do what was once thought impossible.

127
00:20:26,200 --> 00:20:36,940
There is perhaps no better example of this than a patented cloth sold by one company, which remarkably uses only water rather than traditional cleaning products.

128
00:20:36,940 --> 00:20:44,460
The company claims its cloth lifts, traps and removes dirt and bacteria.

129
00:20:44,460 --> 00:20:54,360
And considering that people don't see these products in shops, or advertised on TV or in the press, it has been a pretty steep rise.

130
00:20:54,360 --> 00:21:02,000
Many of the people behind these companies began testing the viability of their products by selling them at local markets.

131
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:12,840
Then, in many cases, customers are doing the marketing for these products by leaving threads of comments on social media, praising their virtues.

132
00:21:12,840 --> 00:21:23,400
There are also TV shows and books dedicated to maintaining home tidiness and cleanliness and the benefits of having a clean home.

133
00:21:23,400 --> 00:21:32,200
Having a safe and clean home might be one thing people feel they can control in an increasingly out-of-control world.

134
00:21:32,200 --> 00:21:35,140
And there are social media celebrities.

135
00:21:35,140 --> 00:21:49,960
One woman in Britain has become a social media sensation with more than two million followers for her cleaning tips page.

136
00:21:49,960 --> 00:22:09,220
Q19. What does the speaker say about the cleaning products industry in recent years?

137
00:22:09,220 --> 00:22:27,320
Q20. Why are newly developed cleaning products selling well?

138
00:22:27,320 --> 00:22:47,300
Q21. What are some customers doing to help promote non-traditional cleaning products?

139
00:22:47,300 --> 00:22:55,900
RECORDING 3
Throwing spare change into a fountain is a time-honored ritual, throw a penny into the water, and your wish might come true.

140
00:22:55,900 --> 00:22:58,660
But all that money has to go somewhere.

141
00:22:58,660 --> 00:23:05,280
Otherwise, the growing piles of pennies, quarters and euros could clog up the fountain's works.

142
00:23:05,280 --> 00:23:09,600
Today, I'm going to talk about where all the coins go.

143
00:23:09,600 --> 00:23:18,080
Well, the coins collected can go to all sorts of different places-from fountain maintenance to charity or public service.

144
00:23:18,080 --> 00:23:32,420
In New York City, for example, coins collected from fountains in public parks often go towards the fountains' maintenance itself, though entrepreneurs who don't mind getting their hands wet often get to it first.

145
00:23:32,420 --> 00:23:38,280
There are over 50 beautiful, decorative display fountains in New York City parks.

146
00:23:38,280 --> 00:23:41,980
They are cleaned by the parks' staff every few weeks.

147
00:23:41,980 --> 00:23:51,300
But most of the coins have already been removed by entrepreneurial New Yorkers and there is not a significant amount left to be collected.

148
00:23:51,300 --> 00:23:56,560
Other cities, though, can pull in a much more serious haul.

149
00:23:56,560 --> 00:24:08,700
Take for example, Rome's famous Trevi fountain, for hundreds of years, visitors have thrown coins over their shoulder into the fountain to ensure that they will return.

150
00:24:08,700 --> 00:24:22,260
So many tourists toss in coins that Roman officials have the fountain cleaned every night, reportedly getting as much as $4,000 in loose change from around the world each day.

151
00:24:22,260 --> 00:24:27,780
Most of the money collected each night goes towards running a supermarket for the needy.

152
00:24:27,780 --> 00:24:31,760
And collecting that cash is serious business.

153
00:24:31,760 --> 00:24:36,940
Roman officials have been known to be tough on anyone caught skimming coins from the fountain.

154
00:24:36,940 --> 00:24:49,140
In one case in 2005, police arrested four fountain cleaners after they were spotted slipping coins into their own pockets after collecting them.

155
00:24:49,140 --> 00:25:03,840
Authorities finally caught one notorious thief named Thomas Morgan and banned him from the fountain after he fished out thousands of dollars in change over 34 years using a magnetic stick.

156
00:25:03,840 --> 00:25:11,520
For the most part, money collected from privately-owned fountains in the United States also goes to charity.

157
00:25:11,520 --> 00:25:24,460
The fountain in New York City's Bryant Park is owned and operated by a not-for-profit corporation, which puts the cash collected by cleaners towards the fountain's own maintenance.

158
00:25:24,460 --> 00:25:37,660
Tens of thousands of dollars in coins removed from wishing wells, fountains and ponds in Florida's Walt Disney World are donated each year to support foster children living in the state.

159
00:25:37,660 --> 00:25:56,020
Whether or not your wish comes true after tossing a coin into a fountain, you can rest assured knowing that the change is likely going to someone who needs it.

160
00:25:56,020 --> 00:26:16,120
Q22. What problem might be caused by the growing piles of coins in fountains?

161
00:26:16,120 --> 00:26:37,520
Q23. What does the speaker say about the coins collected from New York City's park fountains?

162
00:26:37,520 --> 00:26:56,300
Q24. What do we learn about the money collected from Rome's Trevi Fountain?

163
00:26:56,300 --> 00:27:15,631
Q25. What does the speaker say about Thomas Morgan?
